  "# kanban-comment-and-close",
  "",
  "Comment-and-close on a kanban ticket surface: post an audit comment on the ticket named by the work item, then change its state, closing the loop exactly as the surface's runbook prescribes.",
  "",
  "## When to invoke",
  "",
  "Invoke when a work item asks to record the outcome of a ticket on a kanban surface and close it: the item names a ticket, states the effect achieved (or the value to record), and the surface's runbook says the loop is closed with an audit comment followed by a state change. Preconditions:",
  "- The target surface is connected (its slug appears in the connected-surfaces list). If it is not, emit no actions and say so in `notes`.",
  "- The candidate carries a ticket identifier and the value or outcome to record for this run.",
  "- The runbook for the surface names the comment tool, the state-change tool and the exact workflow state to use. Take them from the runbook, never from another team's example.",
  "",
  "## Inputs",
  "",
  "- `<record-id>` — the ticket's identifier on the originating surface, read from the candidate's `Refs:` line or the candidate id. Used as `issueId` on the comment and `id` on the state change.",
  "- `<requested-value>` — the figure, text or outcome the candidate or the runbook names for this run (for example a revised value, a confirmation, or a summary of what was done). It goes into the comment body; it is never a constant in this skill.",
  "- `<reply-channel>` and `<reply-thread>` — the source channel and thread timestamp from the `Reply target:` line, when the work came from a chat channel or thread. Used only for the closing note on the chat surface.",
  "- `<originating-surface>` — the slug of the surface the work came from (for example the kanban surface's slug, or a chat surface's slug). Its runbook says how the loop is closed there: an audit comment then a state change on a ticket, or a reply in the thread on chat.",
  "- `<audit-expectation>` — the read-back the runbook prescribes as evidence that the loop closed: an audit comment visible on the ticket, a returned comment or issue identifier, or a state snapshot. State it in the verification summary.",
  "",
  "## Procedure",
  "",
  "1. Read the candidate. Extract `<record-id>` from the `Refs:` line or candidate id, and `<requested-value>` from the candidate body or the runbook's approved figure for this run. Never invent an identifier, a state name or a result.",
  "2. Read the runbook for `<originating-surface>`. Confirm the tool names for the comment and the state change and the exact workflow-state name to set. On the kanban surface the runbook names `save_comment` for comments and `save_issue` for state changes; the state argument is `state`, not `status`.",
  "3. Draft the audit comment body: one short statement of the observable work completed, including `<requested-value>`, and any values deliberately left unknown. Do not add a provenance trailer or a `username` — the server appends the employee name and run id. Do not include approval-state language (\"queued\", \"pending\", \"awaiting approval\"); describe effects according to the current action mode.",
  "4. Emit the audit comment as its own action (one action per provider mutation, so each can be gated on its own):",
  "",
  "   ```json",
  "   {\"tool\": \"mcp.call\", \"args\": {\"surface\": \"<originating-surface>\", \"tool\": \"save_comment\", \"toolArgsJson\": \"{\\\"issueId\\\": \\\"<record-id>\\\", \\\"body\\\": \\\"<comment body with requested-value>\\\"}\"}}",
  "   ```",
  "",
  "   Send `issueId` and `body` only. Never send `id` or `parentId` — those rewrite or reply to an existing comment; an audit comment is a new top-level comment.",
  "5. In the same response, emit the state change. A status change must always be preceded, in the same response, by the comment on that ticket:",
  "",
  "   ```json",
  "   {\"tool\": \"mcp.call\", \"args\": {\"surface\": \"<originating-surface>\", \"tool\": \"save_issue\", \"toolArgsJson\": \"{\\\"id\\\": \\\"<record-id>\\\", \\\"state\\\": \\\"<workflow state from the runbook>\\\"}\"}}",
  "   ```",
  "",
  "   Use the runbook's completion state (for example the workspace's `Done`) only when the requested effect is complete. A partial result gets the comment and the ticket stays in progress — emit no state change. Do not change assignee, priority, labels or description unless the approved action lists it.",
  "6. If the work came from a chat channel or thread (`<reply-channel>` and `<reply-thread>` are present), emit the closing note to its source channel and thread as its own action, using the chat surface's documented API:",
